<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sithathoriunet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sithathoriunet was an Egyptian princess of the Twelfth Dynasty, probably a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-ii">Senusret II</a>, buried near his pyramid at el-Lahun. Her tomb, excavated by Flinders Petrie and Guy Brunton in 1914, contained a treasure of jewellery, including a gold pectoral bearing the name of Senusret II, now in the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and a crown now in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sithathoriunet.jpg" alt="Sithathoriunet" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyspaosines · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_and_the_Pharaohs%27_Gold,_London_2026-03-06_-_205.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
